 During peak service demand periods, satellite communication systems face the challenge of balancing network efficiency with thermal and power constraints. As demand increases, operators must carefully allocate transponders to maximize capacity without exceeding thermal limits, which can lead to reduced performance or even transponder failure. This requires dynamic and adaptive approaches to load balancing, ensuring that the network operates efficiently while maintaining thermal stability.

Load balancing schedulers, such as the Load Balancing Scheduler mentioned in the context, can help balance the load across multiple carriers, increasing capacity and improving spectral efficiency. These schedulers, however, must also take into account thermal and power constraints to avoid overloading transponders and causing thermal runaway or power failures.

One approach to address this challenge is through dynamic allocation schemes, which can cater to various critical factors, including hot zones, user priority, signal diversity, service types, and even unstable weather. These schemes can allocate more resources to hot beams to meet user demands or address adverse channels, while also considering thermal and power constraints.

In recent years, dynamic spectrum sharing and market-based techniques have been introduced to enhance energy efficiency in satellite systems. These techniques can help manage thermal and power constraints by dynamically adjusting the allocation of transponders based on demand and available resources.

